    "    data[\"Actual_Score\"],\n",
    "    s=60\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Study Hours\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Actual Score\")\n",
    "plt.title(\"Study Hours and Actual Scores\")\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "43ddc1df",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The observations do not lie perfectly on a straight line.\n",
    "\n",
    "Why?\n",
    "\n",
    "Because **study hours are not the only factor determining exam scores**.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## Visualizing the Signal\n",
    "\n",
    "The systematic component of our DGP is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Signal_i = 35 + 5.5(StudyHours_i)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "We can plot this underlying relationship:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "id": "8dbbfb2c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 768x480 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {
      "image/png": {
       "height": 450,
       "width": 659
      }
     },
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "x = data[\"Study_Hours\"]\n",
    "\n",
    "signal = 35 + 5.5 * x\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(8, 5))\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.scatter(\n",
    "    data[\"Study_Hours\"],\n",
    "    data[\"Actual_Score\"],\n",
    "    s=60,\n",
    "    label=\"Observed data\"\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.plot(\n",
    "    x,\n",
    "    signal,\n",
    "    linestyle=\"--\",\n",
    "    label=\"Underlying signal\"\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Study Hours\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Score\")\n",
    "plt.title(\"Signal and Observed Data\")\n",
    "plt.legend()\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "74aad473",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## Signal vs. Noise\n",
    "\n",
    "The **signal** is the systematic pattern that we are interested in.\n",
    "\n",
    "The **noise** is the random variation around that pattern.\n",
    "\n",
    "Conceptually:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\boxed{\n",
    "Y_i = f(X_i) + \\varepsilon_i\n",
    "}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where:\n",
    "\n",
    "* $Y_i$ = observed outcome\n",
    "* $f(X_i)$ = systematic component\n",
    "* $\\varepsilon_i$ = random component\n",
    "\n",
    "In our example:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Score_i\n",
    "=\n",
    "\\underbrace{35 + 5.5(StudyHours_i)}_{\\text{Signal}}\n",
    "+\n",
    "\\underbrace{\\varepsilon_i}_{\\text{Noise}}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "### Key Idea\n",
    "\n",
    "> **We observe the data, but we do not directly observe the DGP.**\n",
    "\n",
    "We try to **infer the underlying process from the observations**.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## Why This Matters for Time Series\n",
    "\n",
    "The same idea applies to time-series data.\n",
    "\n",
    "Instead of:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Score_i = f(StudyHours_i) + \\varepsilon_i\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "we might have:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "Y_t = f(t) + \\varepsilon_t\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where $t$ represents time.\n",
    "\n",
    "The systematic component may contain:\n",
    "\n",
    "* **Level**\n",
    "* **Trend**\n",
    "* **Seasonality**\n",
    "* **Cycles**\n",
    "\n",
    "while the remaining unpredictable variation is the **noise**.\n",
    "\n",
    "This leads to the central objective of time-series analysis:\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Identify the systematic component and separate it from the noise so that we can make better forecasts.**\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "# Naive forecasting Techniques \n",
    "\n",
    "Naive forecasting methods are simple techniques that use historical data to predict future values. These methods are often used when there is limited data available or when the underlying patterns in the data are not well understood. In this section, we will explore some of the most commonly used naive forecasting methods, including the naive methods, moving average methods, exponential smoothing methods, Holt method and Holt-Winters methods. We will also discuss their advantages and limitations, as well as how to implement them in practice.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "# Case 1: We know nothing about the data generating process"
   ]
  }
